<p>South Africa summoned the United States’ new ambassador, Leo Brent Bozell III, to explain comments Pretoria called “undiplomatic,” widening a public dispute that has begun to spill into bilateral ties. The clash follows criticism of South African policies and rhetoric, landing in a sensitive domestic context shaped by the country’s apartheid-era history and ongoing debates over equality measures. After meeting with South Africa’s Department of International Relations and Cooperation, officials said Bozell apologised and expressed regret for the remarks.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/WQVDj5G_Fn6SMRIUlF12bFgfrgsirhd9t89NXwCAWprnOjcX3RBOpmJRoLbPS4K8CJucnFtZ0cxEX0le4_mqd1lJBiCb2o8X4Swr4w21qb2rDxIFevZQa3yDh6SGaMTU41EF7nEqbFdhVGdVJAGKcxa4UU1GG59PKkR5raduxuFPkAk9-B9PnGhD_GQyCfko04vFur30mD1eirQtQAVlIuzZEELVX81of1d0iVLHzQ-LQKQjXJSbPi77rY4l71Xywescjq2ISsw' alt='' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What he said: Bozell faced backlash after calling a South African liberation chant “hate speech,” according to reporting on the remarks that led to the summons.</li><li>Policy criticism: Speaking to business leaders, Bozell challenged South Africa over its diplomatic ties with Iran and its affirmative action laws, PBS reported.</li><li>When it happened: PBS said he made the comments at a business leaders’ meeting on Tuesday; South Africa’s foreign minister said officials summoned him the next day as the rift continued.</li><li>Broader backdrop: The summons came as the dispute widened, with Firstpost linking it to tensions over white Afrikaners and the direction of the two countries’ relationship.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>South Africa’s DIRCO: The department said the U.S. ambassador apologised and expressed regret for remarks it viewed as undiplomatic after meeting South African officials. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202603120101.html'>allAfrica (SAnews.gov.za)</a>)</li><li>U.S. ambassador Leo Brent Bozell III: The BBC reported he weighed in on 